window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('d2',{"s":["<section id=\"d2-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · 2021–2025</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">OBL is the agent; Hanabi tests widen around it</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Hanabi challenge</b> · Bard et al. 2020: a card game where you cannot see your own hand; the ad-hoc teamplay test was left open.</li><li><b>Other-Play</b> · Hu et al. 2020 blocked symmetry conventions for same-method cross-play. OBL (guest) goes further.</li><li><b>Self-play conventions</b> · Self-play agents invent private conventions and can fail with agents trained another way.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Cross-play with copies of one&#x27;s own method is a cheap proxy. How do agents fare with other methods&#x27; partners, a stream of new partners, and humans?</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">OBL grounds the agent. Later papers widen the test: partners from other methods, partners in sequence, the cost of adapting, hidden human proxies.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/d2_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Solid: reused code or test design (InstructRL reuses OBL&#x27;s code). R3D2 joins by topic. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">Zero-shot coordination</b> · play well with a new partner at the first try</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">Inter-XP</b> · cross-play between agents of different methods</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">Adaptation regret</b> · score lost while fine-tuning to a partner</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">Human proxy</b> · an agent trained to imitate human play</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Hanabi and few-shot coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2021–2025 · OBL → Continuous coord. → Few-shot Hanabi → AH2AC2</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The line assumes fixed partners and Hanabi</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#7A2464\">Cooperative Hanabi</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">One shared score. OBL and Few-shot Hanabi are mainly 2-player; R3D2 plays 2-5 players and AH2AC2 2-3.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#7A2464\">Partners do not learn</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Test partners are fixed: pre-trained agents (Continuous coord., Few-shot Hanabi) or frozen human proxies (AH2AC2).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#7A2464\">AI agents stand in for humans</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Five papers score against AI agents or proxies; only InstructRL plays with real people (10 participants).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#7A2464\">Partner pool or none</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Continuous coord. and Few-shot Hanabi build pools of self-play agents; OBL and R3D2 train with no pool, by self-play.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#7A2464\">Relaxed: data and language</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">AH2AC2 allows only 3,079 open human games; InstructRL replaces data with a one-sentence text instruction.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">The Hanabi ad-hoc challenge, Other-Play (the symmetry method OBL improves on), and continual-learning methods (ER, A-GEM, EWC).</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#7A2464\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">OBL grounded agents (23.76 cross-play, 2p); Few-shot Hanabi showed ZSC agents adapt slowly; R3D2 a text-based generalist; AH2AC2 a human-proxy test.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Hanabi and few-shot coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2021–2025 · OBL → Continuous coord. → Few-shot Hanabi → AH2AC2</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">One agent, then ever wider tests and agents on top</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Two papers redefine the test (partner stream, adaptation regret); AH2AC2 reuses it. OBL is the agent three papers compare against; InstructRL reuses its code.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/d2_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:470px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Hanabi and few-shot coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2021–2025 · OBL → Continuous coord. → Few-shot Hanabi → AH2AC2</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Tests for new partners</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2021–2023</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7E3F2;border:2px solid #7A2464;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Continuous coord.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7E3F2;border:2px solid #7A2464;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Few-shot Hanabi</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Lifelong Hanabi feeds fixed partners one by one; Few-shot Hanabi scores adaptation regret over a partner pool.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#7A2464\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Human-proxy benchmark</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2025</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7E3F2;border:2px solid #7A2464;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">AH2AC2</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Proxies trained by cloning, then KL-regularised IPPO, sit behind an API; the small open data gives a few-shot setting.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#7A2464\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Agents: OBL and two more</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2021–2025</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7E3F2;border:2px solid #7A2464;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">OBL</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7E3F2;border:2px solid #7A2464;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">InstructRL</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7E3F2;border:2px solid #7A2464;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">R3D2</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">OBL imagines the past as a fixed policy; InstructRL fine-tunes an OBL level-1 policy toward an LLM prior; R3D2 reads the game as text.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Hanabi and few-shot coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2021–2025 · OBL → Continuous coord. → Few-shot Hanabi → AH2AC2</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · OBL 2021 · 1 of 6 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">OBL: the grounded agent later Hanabi papers use</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Symmetry and belief tree ; here as the agent InstructRL builds on and three tests compare against. Outside: Other-Play, cognitive hierarchies, R2D2.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Turn-based cooperative Dec-POMDP</li><li>A fixed starting policy and a learned belief model</li><li>Unseen partners at test (cross-play)</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reads the past as a fixed policy pi0 and plans the future as its own pi1; repeating gives levels 1-4. No symmetry list needed.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d2_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">InstructRL reuses its code and level-1 policy; Few-shot Hanabi, R3D2 and AH2AC2 compare against it as a baseline.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi 2p cross-play: level 4 23.76/25 vs Other-play 21.77 (Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Off-Belief Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hu et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Continuous coord. 2021 · 2 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Meeting new partners makes Hanabi a lifelong RL test</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: the Hanabi challenge, ER/A-GEM/EWC, IRM. Oldest home member: no parent inside this tree.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Fixed pre-trained partners, met one at a time</li><li>A pool of 100+ pre-trained self-play agents</li><li>No known symmetries, no peek at partner action</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Treats each fixed partner as a new task for one continual learner, scored zero-shot and few-shot on unseen agents.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d2_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Few-shot Hanabi reuses its cross-play over pre-trained partners and turns its brief few-shot test into adaptation regret.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Lifelong Hanabi: zero-shot with unseen agents 19.17/25 (IQL+AUX+ER) vs SAD 14.60 (Table 2, Inter-CP).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Continuous Coordination As a Realistic Scenario for Lifelong Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Nekoei et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · InstructRL 2023 · 3 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Language instructions steer RL to human-like partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses OBL&#x27;s codebase and fine-tunes an OBL level-1 policy (OBL is a guest here). Outside: human-regularised RL, LLM action scoring.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>A human can state the strategy in a sentence</li><li>One fixed instruction; no human feedback in training</li><li>Observations and actions can be put in text</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A pretrained LLM turns the instruction into a prior policy that regularizes Q-learning or PPO toward the matching equilibrium.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d2_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">R3D2 cites it in related work and also reads Hanabi as text, but shares no method; no solid edge out.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi 2p with 10 humans: 18.70 with instruction vs 9.80 for Q-learning (Table 4).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Language Instructed Reinforcement Learning for Human-AI Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hu et al. · ICML 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Few-shot Hanabi 2023 · 4 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Strong ZSC agents still adapt slowly to new partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends Continuous coord.: reuses its cross-play over pre-trained partners. Compares against OBL (guest) and Other-Play as learners. Outside: Hanabi ad-hoc challenge.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player cooperative Hanabi</li><li>Fixed, strong, diverse held-out partners</li><li>A learner that fine-tunes on each partner</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Defines adaptation regret: score lost while a learner fine-tunes on a fixed partner from a diverse pool.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d2_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">AH2AC2 takes its few-shot framing into data-limited human-proxy settings; R3D2 lists it as future work.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi, partner set D 0.62: OBL agent 24 with itself, 0 to 9 with IQL/VDN partners (Fig. 3b).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Towards Few-Shot Coordination: Revisiting Ad-Hoc Teamplay Challenge In the Game of Hanabi</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Nekoei et al. · CoLLAs 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · R3D2 2025 · 5 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">One Hanabi agent plays 2-5 players and new partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: R2D2, DRRN, TinyBERT. Compares against OBL (guest) and Other-Play; cites InstructRL, Continuous coord., Few-shot only as related work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Self-play training, no partner pool</li><li>Game state and actions readable as text</li><li>Partners are other AI agents</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Writes the state and each action as text, scores actions with a TinyBERT+LSTM relevance network, trains by plain self-play.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d2_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi 2p inter-XP about 11.6 vs about 7 to 8.4 for IQL, Other-Play, OBL; one agent for 2-5 players.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">A Generalist Hanabi Agent</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Sudhakar et al. · ICLR 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · AH2AC2 2025 · 6 of 6</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A Hanabi benchmark for coordinating with human proxies</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends Few-shot Hanabi&#x27;s few-shot framing. Compares against OBL (guest), its best baseline. Outside: Hanabi challenge, human-regularised RL.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Fixed human proxies as partners</li><li>Little open human data (3,079 games)</li><li>One 1,000-game evaluation run per setting</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Trains human proxies by cloning then KL-regularised IPPO, hides them behind an API with a leaderboard.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/d2_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi 2P hidden proxies: proxies 22.76; best baseline OBL 21.04 with no human data (1,000-game mean).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Ad-Hoc Human-AI Coordination Challenge</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Dizdarević et al. · ICML 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Each paper tests a wider kind of partner</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi score out of 25: cross-play with unseen AI partners (Continuous coord., Few-shot Hanabi, R3D2).</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Humans enter via 10 people (InstructRL) or hidden proxies (AH2AC2).</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Baselines include SAD, Other-Play, OBL, IQL, VDN; each paper picks its own.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#F7E3F2;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Different benchmarks and partners: do not rank rows. Read each row against its own baseline.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">OBL</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p cross-play (Table 1)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Level 4: 23.76/25 vs Other-play 21.77</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">Continuous coord.</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Lifelong Hanabi, unseen agents</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">IQL+AUX+ER 19.17/25 vs SAD 14.60</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">InstructRL</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p, 10 humans</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">18.70 with instruction vs 9.80 Q-learning</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">Few-shot Hanabi</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi cross-play, partner set D 0.62</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">OBL: 24 with itself, 0 to 9 with IQL/VDN</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">R3D2</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p inter-XP</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">About 11.6 vs about 7 to 8.4 baselines</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#7A2464\">AH2AC2</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Hanabi 2p, hidden human proxies</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">OBL 21.04; proxies 22.76 (reference)</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Hanabi and few-shot coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2021–2025 · OBL → Continuous coord. → Few-shot Hanabi → AH2AC2</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"d2-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A2464\">Hanabi few-shot · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #7A2464;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#7A2464;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Adapting to a new partner still takes millions of episodes (Few-shot Hanabi).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #7A2464;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#7A2464;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Human evidence is small (10 people) or proxy-based; AH2AC2 covers only 2-3 players.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #7A2464;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#7A2464;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">R3D2 trades strategy strength for generality; Other-Play and OBL baselines exist only for 2 players.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Hanabi and few-shot coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">6 papers (1 guests) · 2021–2025 · OBL → Continuous coord. → Few-shot Hanabi → AH2AC2</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Tree d2, 6 members: OBL (2021, guest from tree d1 &#x27;Symmetry and belief&#x27;), Continuous coord. (2021), InstructRL (2023), Few-shot Hanabi (2023), R3D2 (2025), AH2AC2 (2025). Edges classified from the child&#x27;s text. Solid (kind 1): OBL -&gt; InstructRL (InstructRL reuses the OBL codebase and fine-tunes an OBL level-1 policy); Continuous coord. -&gt; Few-shot Hanabi (reuses cross-play over pre-trained partners and the brief few-shot test, adds adaptation regret); Few-shot Hanabi -&gt; AH2AC2 (takes the few-shot framing, adds data-limited settings). No edge is kind 0. Comparison/background (kind 2): Few-shot Hanabi, R3D2 and AH2AC2 -&gt; OBL (OBL is a baseline or a benchmarked learner), R3D2 -&gt; InstructRL (related work), R3D2 -&gt; Continuous coord. (related work, population training), R3D2 -&gt; Few-shot Hanabi (future work only). What the guest changes: OBL was previously only &#x27;outside the tree&#x27;. Now InstructRL has a real parent, and the tree shows OBL as the agent that the test papers measure. R3D2 still has no solid edge and joins by topic and shared authorship (Nekoei). Continuous coord. and OBL are not linked (both 2021, no citation edge in the data). Background from the members&#x27; related work: Bard et al. 2020 (Hanabi challenge), Hu et al. 2020a (Other-Play), Treutlein et al. 2021 (label-free coordination), Lupu et al. 2021 and Lucas &amp; Allen 2022 (diverse populations, Anyplay). Survey vocabulary: Mirsky et al. ST4 (adapting to new partners) for the two test papers; OBL is zero-shot coordination (Albrecht and Stone: closest to type-free grounded reasoning); the other agents and AH2AC2 sit outside the surveys&#x27; core.","Shared assumptions, per paper. OBL (guest): turn-based cooperative Dec-POMDP, a fixed starting policy known to all, a learned belief model, unseen partners at test; 2-player main results. Continuous coord.: fixed pre-trained partners met one at a time. Few-shot Hanabi: 2-player, fixed strong diverse partners, fine-tuning learner. R3D2: self-play training, state readable as text, AI partners; 2-5 players. AH2AC2: fixed human proxies, 3,079 open games (about 2% of 147,621 hidden, own arithmetic), one 1,000-game run, 2-3 players, H-group conventions. InstructRL: a human states the strategy in text, one fixed instruction, 10 human participants. Relaxations named in the slide: AH2AC2 limits data on purpose (data-limited, offline one-sided adaptation); InstructRL replaces data with a text instruction; R3D2 drops the partner pool and the 2-player limit. All assumptions come from the member notes; where a note said &#x27;not spelled out&#x27; (InstructRL training pairing, Continuous coord. 5 vs 10 tasks), the point is left out.","Stage 1 (tests for new partners): Continuous coord. -&gt; Few-shot Hanabi (kind 1). Lifelong Hanabi treats each fixed partner as a task for one continual learner; Few-shot Hanabi defines adaptation regret R_T(i, j) = T * C*_j - E[sum of scores], aggregated over a partner pool (mean or IQM), including the learner&#x27;s zero-shot start. Stage 2 (human-proxy benchmark): Few-shot Hanabi -&gt; AH2AC2 (kind 1). Proxies: behavioural cloning of hanab.live games, then IPPO with a KL term to the BC policy (L = (1 - lambda) L_IPPO + lambda KL(BC || proxy)); hidden behind an API with a private key (one 1,000-game run) and a leaderboard. Stage 3 (agents): OBL (guest) reads the past as a fixed policy pi0 and plans the future with its own pi1 (counterfactual Q-values, iterated to levels 1-4; R2D2 backbone, learned belief over the hidden hand). InstructRL (kind 1 from OBL, code and level-1 policy) regularizes Q-learning or PPO toward an LLM prior from a text instruction; R3D2 writes the state and actions as text, scores them with a TinyBERT+LSTM relevance network and trains by self-play. R3D2 is not inherited from OBL, InstructRL or the test papers (R3D2 cites them only as related work or future work). Sources: member notes and slides p37, p105, p90, p107, p53, p117.","Stage 1 (tests for new partners): Continuous coord. -&gt; Few-shot Hanabi (kind 1). Lifelong Hanabi treats each fixed partner as a task for one continual learner; Few-shot Hanabi defines adaptation regret R_T(i, j) = T * C*_j - E[sum of scores], aggregated over a partner pool (mean or IQM), including the learner&#x27;s zero-shot start. Stage 2 (human-proxy benchmark): Few-shot Hanabi -&gt; AH2AC2 (kind 1). Proxies: behavioural cloning of hanab.live games, then IPPO with a KL term to the BC policy (L = (1 - lambda) L_IPPO + lambda KL(BC || proxy)); hidden behind an API with a private key (one 1,000-game run) and a leaderboard. Stage 3 (agents): OBL (guest) reads the past as a fixed policy pi0 and plans the future with its own pi1 (counterfactual Q-values, iterated to levels 1-4; R2D2 backbone, learned belief over the hidden hand). InstructRL (kind 1 from OBL, code and level-1 policy) regularizes Q-learning or PPO toward an LLM prior from a text instruction; R3D2 writes the state and actions as text, scores them with a TinyBERT+LSTM relevance network and trains by self-play. R3D2 is not inherited from OBL, InstructRL or the test papers (R3D2 cites them only as related work or future work). Sources: member notes and slides p37, p105, p90, p107, p53, p117.","Guest paper. Home: tree d1 &#x27;Symmetry and belief&#x27; . Paper: Hu, Lerer, Cui, Pineda, Brown, Foerster; ICML 2021. It sits in this tree because of one inheritance edge and three comparison edges. Edges: InstructRL -&gt; OBL (kind 1): InstructRL implements instructQ/instructPPO on the OBL open-source codebase and fine-tunes an OBL level-1 policy instead of training from scratch (Sec. on implementation, Appendix A.2); its method (LLM prior) is different. Few-shot Hanabi -&gt; OBL (kind 2): OBL is benchmarked as a learner adapting to unseen partners, next to IQL and VDN; the paper finds naive IQL adapts about as fast as OBL. R3D2 -&gt; OBL (kind 2): R2D2-OBL baseline, level 4, original 2-player checkpoints. AH2AC2 -&gt; OBL (kind 2): pre-trained OBL (L4) agents are the strongest baseline. Numbers (Table 1, 2-player Hanabi, 5 seeds, 5,000 games per model): cross-play SAD 2.52 (not directly comparable, other network), K-Level 17.17, Other-play 21.77, OBL L1 20.85, L2 23.24, L3 23.68, L4 23.76. Self-play: OBL L1 20.92, L4 24.10, Other-play 24.14. With human-data Clone Bot: OBL 13.80 to 16.02 vs Other-play 8.55. Toy game: OBL level 1 earns +5 in every pairing, self-play agents +10 or -10. How the tree papers report it (own benchmarks, not comparable to Table 1): Few-shot Hanabi Fig. 3b, OBL 24 with itself and 0 to 9 with IQL/VDN partners; R3D2 Fig. 4 inter-XP about 7.0; AH2AC2 Table 5, 2P hidden proxies 21.04 (no 3-player weights). Caveats: below Other-play in self-play at level 1; needs a belief model and game rules; no human study (Clone Bot is a proxy); not extended to zero-sum games. Step names in the home slides are the notes&#x27; own grouping.","Paper: Nekoei, Badrinaaraayanan, Courville, Chandar; ICML 2021. Oldest home member of the tree (OBL, a guest, is also 2021); no parent edge. It is the parent of Few-shot Hanabi (kind 1: reused cross-play over pre-trained partners; Few-shot Hanabi&#x27;s related work says Nekoei et al. 2021 propose inter-cross-play evaluation and mention few-shot evaluation briefly) and is cited by R3D2 in related work (kind 2: population training with diverse policies). Numbers: Table 2 Inter-CP (20 agents across all MARL methods, score out of 25): SAD 14.60, SAD+AUX 18.51, SAD+OP 16.09, SAD+AUX+OP 17.77, IQL+ER 16.32, IQL+AUX+ER 19.17, IQL+Multi-task 17.88. Self-play 23.85, intra-CP 7.70 for SAD. Table 1 (hard task, 5 partners, Adam, zero-shot average, scores mapped to 0-1): naive 0.39, online EWC 0.55, ER 0.44, A-GEM 0.38, multi-task 0.70. Forgetting lowest for ER with SGD (0.05). Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST4 (adapting across partners, lifelong learning). Caveats: Table 2 SAD rows are pre-trained comparison methods, not lifelong learners. The 5 vs 10 task setting behind Table 2 learners is not stated in the main text; PDF has 9 pages, no appendices. Partners are fixed (learning partners left for future work). Table 1 column labels AT/FT were read as average score / forgetting.","Paper: Hu and Sadigh; ICML 2023. One solid edge in: OBL (guest, kind 1): it implements instructQ/instructPPO on the OBL open-source codebase and fine-tunes an OBL level-1 policy; the LLM-prior method is its own. No kind 0/1 edge out. R3D2 cites it (kind 2): R3D2&#x27;s related work says Hu and Sadigh propose a framework where the partner specifies an expected strategy, with a prior policy generated by an LLM, but conditioned only on other agents&#x27; actions and heuristic instructions; R3D2 also says Hu and Sadigh support that large LLMs are far from solving Hanabi alone. Numbers: Table 4 (10 humans, mean +- s.e., games lost of 10): Q-learning 9.80 +- 3.35, 5/10 lost; InstructQ without showing the instruction 7.80 +- 3.23, 6/10; InstructQ with instruction 18.70 +- 2.18, 1/10. Self-play (Table 3): Q-learning 23.96, InstructQ color 23.78, rank 23.92. Say-Select toy game: instructQ found the intuitive policy in 10 of 10 seeds. LLMs: GPT-J 6B (Say-Select), GPT-3.5 (Hanabi). Survey vocabulary: outside both surveys (LLM era); zero-shot human-AI coordination. Caveats: only 10 participants, large standard errors. The instruction is hand-written and fixed. Test-time adaptation left to future work. Hanabi training pairing not spelled out. It joins the line through OBL (code reuse), not through the test papers.","Paper: Nekoei, Zhao, Rajendran, Liu, Chandar; CoLLAs 2023. Edge in: Continuous coord. (kind 1): it reuses Lifelong Hanabi&#x27;s inter-cross-play evaluation of agents against pre-trained partners; the paper says Nekoei et al. 2021 mention few-shot evaluation only briefly and that none of those approaches evaluates adaptation of pre-trained agents comprehensively. The method (adaptation regret) is new. Comparison edge (kind 2): OBL (guest) is benchmarked as a learner adapting to unseen partners, with IQL and VDN; the paper finds naive IQL adapts about as fast as OBL. Edges out: AH2AC2 (kind 1: &#x27;To connect AH2AC2 with few-shot coordination, we introduce data-limited settings&#x27;), R3D2 (kind 2: future work only). Numbers: Fig. 3(b), partner set with diversity 0.62, strength 0.94: OBL5 row reads 8 (IQL), 9 (IQL_OP), 3 (VDN), 0 (VDN_OP), 24 with itself (single agent, integer cell values). Partner sets D 0.48 / 0.62 / 0.78, S 0.94 / 0.94 / 0.96. Findings: all learners need millions of episodes; naive IQL adapts about as fast as OBL; IQL and VDN beat their Other-play counterparts, so successful ZSC techniques do not automatically help adaptation. Tuning: threads ~20, buffer ~5e4. Code: chandar-lab/adaptive-hanabi (link from the paper, not checked). Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST4 (adaptation speed, evaluation); outside Albrecht-Stone method families. Caveats: no results table and no single headline number (the headline is the Fig. 3b row); regret curves were not read off plots; the &#x27;20 threads&#x27; wording is ambiguous.","Paper: Sudhakar, Nekoei et al.; ICLR 2025 (Nekoei is also first author of the two Hanabi test papers in this tree). Edges (all kind 2, none of them inheritance): OBL (guest; R2D2-OBL baseline, level 4, original 2-player checkpoints; R3D2 pairs better with R2D2-OBL than any other baseline, per its text), InstructRL (related work: LLM prior from a text instruction; also supports that LLMs alone cannot play Hanabi), Continuous coord. (related work: population training with diverse policies), Few-shot Hanabi (only suggested as future work: &#x27;Exploring few-shot coordination could be a promising research direction&#x27;). The method comes from outside the tree: R2D2, DRRN, TinyBERT. Numbers: read off the bars of Fig. 4 (2 players, 3 seeds), approximate to about 0.3. SP / intra-XP / inter-XP: IQL 23.7 / 15.8 / 8.1; Other-Play 23.3 / 12.7 / 8.4; OBL 24.2 / 24.0 / 7.0; R3D2-S 23.7 / 23.7 / 11.6; R3D2-M 21.1 / 20.7 / 11.3. Both R3D2 variants stay above 15 in 3 to 5 player settings (Appendix A.1). GPT-4 prompting about 3 points, LoRA LLaMA-7B about 1. Survey vocabulary: outside Mirsky ST1-ST4 core (self-play agent generalizing across player counts); ZSC lineage. Caveats: no table in the paper; OP and OBL baselines exist only for 2 players; inter-XP pairs different algorithms. Strategies are traded for generality (R3D2-M self-play lower). No humans, no adaptation.","Paper: Dizdarevic et al.; ICML 2025. Edge in: Few-shot Hanabi (kind 1): the paper says Nekoei et al. (2023) urge the community to address few-shot adaptation alongside ZSC, that current ZSC algorithms struggle to adapt, and &#x27;to connect AH2AC2 with few-shot coordination, we introduce data-limited settings&#x27;, in an offline, one-sided form (the proxies stay fixed at test time). The mechanism (behavioural cloning then KL-regularised IPPO) is different from the parent&#x27;s. OBL (guest) is a baseline only (kind 2): pre-trained L4 agents, 2-player only. Numbers: Table 5, 2-player mean score with hidden human proxies: Human proxies 22.76 (reference), OBL 21.04, BR-BC 19.41, FCP 14.01, OP 13.91, HDR-IPPO 12.76, IPPO 10.16, DeepSeek-R1 with H-group prompt 9.91 (100 games), DeepSeek-R1 5.43, BC 2.12. 3-player: proxies 20.86, DeepSeek-R1 H-Group 14.62 (100 games), HDR-IPPO 14.03, OP 12.87; OBL has no 3-player weights. Data: 147,621 hidden human games vs 3,079 open (about 2%, own arithmetic; the 3,079 include validation sets per Appendix A.4). BR-BC trained on the entire dataset reaches 22.59 (2P), 18.80 (3P): the small open set looks like the bottleneck (inference in the notes, not the paper&#x27;s claim). Code: github.com/FLAIROx/ah2ac2 (not checked). Survey vocabulary: evaluation domain with human-proxy partners; outside Albrecht-Stone. Caveats: the proxy bar is a reference, not a competitor; methods trained on open data trail OBL (zero-shot, no human data). Only 2 and 3 players, H-group conventions only. Sec. 6 says HDR-IPPO leads 3P, yet DeepSeek-R1 (100 games only) scores higher.","Numbers are as written in each paper&#x27;s own notes and are not comparable across rows. OBL (guest): Table 1, 2p Hanabi, cross-play L4 23.76, Other-play 21.77, K-Level 17.17, SAD* 2.52 (not directly comparable); with Clone Bot 13.80 to 16.02 vs Other-play 8.55. Continuous coord.: Table 2 Inter-CP, IQL+AUX+ER 19.17/25, SAD 14.60, SAD+AUX 18.51, SAD+OP 16.09. InstructRL: Table 4, 10 humans, 18.70 +- 2.18 with instruction vs 9.80 +- 3.35 Q-learning, 7.80 +- 3.23 without showing instruction. Few-shot Hanabi: Fig. 3(b) OBL5 row 8 / 9 / 3 / 0 with IQL / IQL_OP / VDN / VDN_OP, 24 with itself. R3D2: Fig. 4 bars, approx., inter-XP R3D2-S 11.6, R3D2-M 11.3 vs IQL 8.1, Other-Play 8.4, OBL 7.0. AH2AC2: Table 5, 2P proxies 22.76, OBL 21.04, BR-BC 19.41, FCP 14.01, OP 13.91, HDR-IPPO 12.76, IPPO 10.16. Why no ranking: the score scales are all out of 25, but the partners differ (self-play pool, 10 humans, hidden proxies, other algorithms), so a higher number does not mean a better paper. OBL&#x27;s own cross-play numbers use its own partners; the same agent scores 21.04 against hidden proxies in AH2AC2 and about 7.0 inter-XP in R3D2: different partners, not comparable. Open points from the notes: Few-shot Hanabi (millions of episodes to adapt), InstructRL (10 participants), R3D2 (2-player baselines only), AH2AC2 (2-3 players, H-group only).","Numbers are as written in each paper&#x27;s own notes and are not comparable across rows. OBL (guest): Table 1, 2p Hanabi, cross-play L4 23.76, Other-play 21.77, K-Level 17.17, SAD* 2.52 (not directly comparable); with Clone Bot 13.80 to 16.02 vs Other-play 8.55. Continuous coord.: Table 2 Inter-CP, IQL+AUX+ER 19.17/25, SAD 14.60, SAD+AUX 18.51, SAD+OP 16.09. InstructRL: Table 4, 10 humans, 18.70 +- 2.18 with instruction vs 9.80 +- 3.35 Q-learning, 7.80 +- 3.23 without showing instruction. Few-shot Hanabi: Fig. 3(b) OBL5 row 8 / 9 / 3 / 0 with IQL / IQL_OP / VDN / VDN_OP, 24 with itself. R3D2: Fig. 4 bars, approx., inter-XP R3D2-S 11.6, R3D2-M 11.3 vs IQL 8.1, Other-Play 8.4, OBL 7.0. AH2AC2: Table 5, 2P proxies 22.76, OBL 21.04, BR-BC 19.41, FCP 14.01, OP 13.91, HDR-IPPO 12.76, IPPO 10.16. Why no ranking: the score scales are all out of 25, but the partners differ (self-play pool, 10 humans, hidden proxies, other algorithms), so a higher number does not mean a better paper. OBL&#x27;s own cross-play numbers use its own partners; the same agent scores 21.04 against hidden proxies in AH2AC2 and about 7.0 inter-XP in R3D2: different partners, not comparable. Open points from the notes: Few-shot Hanabi (millions of episodes to adapt), InstructRL (10 participants), R3D2 (2-player baselines only), AH2AC2 (2-3 players, H-group only)."]});
